{
  "query": "scrumtuous.com operates a global streaming platform and uses BigQuery to analyze event data. They require real time insights for plays and purchases, and they have major audiences in the United States, the European Union, and Asia Pacific. Your architecture must keep data in region and deliver very low latency queries for teams in each geography. Which approach should you implement?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use BigQuery multi region and partition tables by a region field",
      "explanation": "This keeps data in a multi region location and uses logical partitioning to separate regional data.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Adopt BigQuery Omni to analyze data in each geography across multiple clouds",
      "explanation": "This allows querying data where it resides in non GCP clouds without moving it.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Place a single BigQuery dataset in the US and schedule BigQuery Data Transfer Service runs from the EU and APAC every 45 minutes",
      "explanation": "This centralizes analytics in the US and uses scheduled transfers to replicate other regions&apos; data.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a dedicated BigQuery dataset in each region and query across datasets when needed",
      "explanation": "This stores each region&apos;s data in its local dataset and still permits cross dataset queries for global analysis.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Create a dedicated BigQuery dataset in each region and query across datasets when needed</b>.</p><p>This approach satisfies data residency by keeping each geography’s data inside its regional BigQuery location. Query execution occurs in the same location as the data which preserves in region requirements and yields very low latency for local analysts. You can enable collaboration by sharing curated results across regions using services like Analytics Hub or by copying aggregated tables on a schedule while keeping primary ingestion and interactive queries local to each region.</p><p><i>Use BigQuery multi region and partition tables by a region field</i> is not appropriate because a multi region location does not guarantee that APAC data remains in APAC and a partition column does not determine physical data location. This choice can introduce unnecessary cross ocean latency and can conflict with strict residency needs.</p><p><i>Adopt BigQuery Omni to analyze data in each geography across multiple clouds</i> is designed for analyzing data stored in other clouds such as AWS and Azure. It adds complexity and cost without solving this Google Cloud only scenario and it does not provide managed BigQuery storage and execution per GCP region for this use case.</p><p><i>Place a single BigQuery dataset in the US and schedule BigQuery Data Transfer Service runs from the EU and APAC every 45 minutes</i> breaks data residency for the EU and APAC and it does not provide real time insights. The Data Transfer Service is built for scheduled batch transfers rather than low latency streaming analytics.</p>",
